## Context

The scaffolded slash commands are the only mechanism by which the
maintainer's documented pipeline phases become actual runnable
agent sessions inside a host project. The docs describe the pipeline;
the commands execute it. Without this helper, the pipeline exists
only as documentation, and a developer adopting AIDE would have to
recreate each phase by hand. That is the cold-start gap the parent
spec exists to eliminate, and this helper is one of the two places
(along with writeMethodology) where that elimination actually
happens.

Each command file is small and discrete — one pipeline phase, one
checklist — but together they are load-bearing: delete any one of
them and the pipeline has a hole. The phase set is defined by the
canonical docs, and no agent, command, or human can dilute it by
adding an overlapping phase without updating the canonical templates
and the parent spec to justify the addition.

The study-playbook skill and the update-playbook command form a
complementary pair around the coding playbook. The skill teaches
agents how to navigate any playbook hub top-down — generic crawling
mechanics that work regardless of what sections the user has. The
update-playbook command gives humans a way to evolve the playbook's
content and structure, with a built-in housekeeping step that keeps
the hub's task routing table in sync with the actual sections. Task
routing — the mapping from task domains to playbook sections — lives
exclusively in the playbook hub note, not in the skill. This
separation means the skill never needs updating when the user
reorganizes their playbook; the routing table in the hub is the
single source of truth for which section serves which task domain.

## Strategy

**Templates read from docs/commands/aide/ at runtime through
initContent.** The canonical command templates live as Markdown
files under docs/commands/aide/, one per invocable phase. This
helper asks initContent for each template by canonical name,
receives the file's bytes verbatim, applies the framework-specific
wrapper if any, and writes the result into an `aide/` subfolder of
the host's command directory. A template edit in docs/commands/aide/
propagates automatically on the next init run in every host project.

**Install into an `aide/` subfolder, never flat.** Host frameworks
that support slash commands treat nested folders as namespace
prefixes: a file at `<command-dir>/aide/research.md` becomes
`/aide:research`. Installing to the command directory root with a
dash-prefixed filename (`aide-research.md`) is the wrong layout —
it would break the namespace grouping the canonical command-hub
spec requires and double-name the phase at every reference.

**Existing files are immutable.** If the target path already has a
command file, the helper reports `exists` and moves on. It does not
compare content, diff against the template, or offer to upgrade.
The user may have customized the command — for their team's
workflow, for their domain, for debugging. Customizations are
first-class and survive re-runs unconditionally. A future explicit
`aide_upgrade` path can handle intentional re-sync; this helper
never performs one by accident.

**Commands are enumerated from a fixed registry, not discovered dynamically.**
The helper iterates a fixed COMMANDS registry: the orchestrator (`aide.md` at
the command directory root) plus subcommands under `aide/`. The registry
membership spans pipeline phase commands, lifecycle commands, and maintenance
commands; the canonical command-hub spec is the source of truth for what
belongs in the set. Adding a new command requires adding a canonical template
under docs/commands/aide/, updating the parent spec to justify the addition,
and extending the registry here. All three changes land in the same PR or none
of them do — this is the three-way lock the canonical command-hub spec declares
mandatory. A helper that discovers its command list dynamically from the docs
folder is a regression, because it lets a stray Markdown file in the wrong
place silently expand the pipeline.

**The study-playbook skill is a generic crawling protocol.** It teaches
agents the mechanics of navigating any playbook hub top-down: what a hub
note is, how to traverse wikilinks, the fan-out depth rules, the
never-re-read invariant. It does not contain an intent mapping table or
any reference to specific playbook section names. Task routing — which
section to read for which task domain — lives in the playbook hub note
itself as a task routing table. When the skill tells the agent to read
the hub, the agent sees the routing table at the top and knows where to
go. This separation means the skill works for any user's playbook
structure without modification.

**The update-playbook command includes drift detection as a required
step.** When a user adds, renames, or removes playbook sections, the
task routing table in the hub can silently fall out of sync. The
update-playbook command's housekeeping step instructs the agent to
compare the routing table against the actual section structure and offer
to reconcile any drift — adding new rows for new sections, flagging
stale rows that point to removed sections, and suggesting
reorganization when sections have shifted domains.

**Per-command status, not a single aggregate.** Each install reports
its own result. The init summary shows a status per scaffolded command — one
line per COMMANDS entry, in the form `<displayName>: <status>` — so a
developer reading the output knows exactly which files are new on this run.
A single aggregate status hides the mix and forces the developer to inspect
the directory manually.

## Good examples

A host project runs the install action for the first time —
through the `aide_init` MCP tool or the npx CLI. The scaffold helper
reads templates from docs/commands/aide/ through initContent, writes
`aide.md` at the command directory root (the orchestrator) and the
subcommand files into `.claude/commands/aide/` (or the equivalent
for the detected framework) — one file per entry in the COMMANDS
registry — and returns a `created` status for each. The install
summary lists each command by name with its status. The developer
can immediately type `/aide:research` in their next session and the
pipeline begins.

A developer has customized `.claude/commands/aide/build.md` to add
project-specific build steps. They re-run the install action to
pick up a new MCP server registration. The scaffold helper sees the
existing build file, reports `exists`, and leaves the customization
intact. The other commands in the registry also report `exists`. The
developer's customization survived, and the re-run was safe — same
result through either entry point.

A maintainer edits the canonical research template in
docs/commands/aide/research.md to clarify the "conflicts resolved"
checklist item. They release a new version of the MCP server. A
fresh host project runs the install action; the new template text
lands in the new project's `aide/research.md` command file on first
install. A host project that was already initialized before the
edit does not automatically pick up the change — its existing file
is preserved — and a future explicit upgrade path is the correct
mechanism for intentional re-sync.

A developer runs `/aide:update-playbook` to add a new "error
handling" section to their coding playbook. The agent adds the
section, then runs the housekeeping step: it compares the playbook
hub's task routing table against the actual sections and notices
"error handling" has no routing entry. It offers to add a row
mapping the relevant task domains to the new section. The routing
table stays in sync without the developer having to remember it
exists.

A developer reads the study-playbook skill and sees only generic
crawling instructions: read the hub, follow relevant wikilinks
top-down, fan out 1-2 levels, never re-read. There is no mention
of specific playbook sections like "naming conventions" or "test
patterns" — those details live in the playbook hub's task routing
table, which the agent discovers naturally when it reads the hub.

## Bad examples

An installed command file that contains "Search for existing
research: vault notes, transcripts, external sources" when the
current canonical template in docs/ says "Search the brain, then
external sources, then team notes, in that order". The scaffold
helper was composing from a template literal baked into its source
instead of reading from docs/. The host project's pipeline now
teaches a stale workflow; the maintainer has no way to know.

A re-run that overwrites a developer's customized `aide/qa.md`
with the canonical template "because the canonical is authoritative".
The customization — which added a team-specific audit checklist —
is gone, the developer notices only when their next QA session
feels wrong, and trust in the tool is broken.

A scaffold helper that installs flat dash-prefixed files like
`.claude/commands/aide-research.md` into the command directory root
instead of `.claude/commands/aide/research.md`. The host's slash
command becomes `/aide-research` instead of `/aide:research`, the
namespace grouping is gone, and every other scaffolded phase
collides with anything else the host has at the top level of the
command directory. The folder-as-namespace layout was the whole
contract with the canonical command-hub spec, and it was skipped.

A scaffold helper that walks the docs/commands/aide/ directory at
runtime and installs every .md file it finds. A stray scratch file
committed to docs/commands/aide/ accidentally ships as an extra
slash command. The fixed-list invariant was enforced nowhere, the
pipeline dilutes silently, and nobody notices until an agent runs
`/aide:wip` expecting something real.

A scaffold helper that catches a missing template for one command
and aborts the whole step, returning zero installs. The other six
commands could have been written successfully; instead the host
project has a broken init and no pipeline at all, because one
template was misplaced. Independent per-command reporting was the
whole point.

A study-playbook skill that contains a table like "naming → read
conventions/naming.md, testing → read conventions/testing.md". This
is environment-specific routing baked into a generic skill. A user
whose playbook organizes conventions differently gets wrong
directions from the skill. The routing belongs in the playbook hub
note, where the user controls it.

An update-playbook command that lets the user add and modify
playbook sections but has no housekeeping step. The user adds three
new sections over a month, the task routing table still points to
the original structure, and agents using study-playbook navigate to
stale or missing sections. The drift was preventable — the command
should have detected it and offered to reconcile.
